From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 06:28:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] PUBLISH-FIX-117: the firehose is drained in bounded turns,
 never in one
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The FIX-116 meter named the block in its first minute on 3fc737d3:
  TAPE TURN: 6321 messages dispatched in ONE turn of the loop, 1971ms
  TAPE TURN: 6529 messages dispatched in ONE turn of the loop, 2844ms
The socket hands the parser everything it buffered and every message is
dispatched before the loop runs anything else; after any pause that is
seconds of firehose, the drain takes seconds, more arrives meanwhile, the
next turn is bigger. Timers, database answers (the hypothesis lock), the
Judge's decision queue and the watchdog heartbeat all waited inside it —
the ~13 s blocks, the LAG_SUSTAINED sheds and `hypothesis lock not durable
within 10 s` were made of this, not of CPU percent and not of the collector
(FIX-115 measured that).

lib/turn-inbox.js: an ordered inbox drained in slices of at most 50 ms of
wall time (the main-lane budget), yielding to the loop between slices.
Nothing dropped, nothing reordered; a message queued by a socket that has
since closed is skipped (an old epoch's update must never touch a new book;
the venue re-sends a snapshot on reconnect). tape/run.js pushes the socket's
messages through it and reports the deepest backlog and longest slice once a
minute. Re-pinned (SCOPE-5). Authority NONE: dispatch timing only.

